-- title:
--   Two-point global-to-local zeta factorisation at a bad place
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field of degree $3$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, with $\mathcal{O}_K$ an integral $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra; let $\psi$ be a global additive character of the adeles of $\mathbb{Q}$ (trivial on principal adeles, continuous, non-trivial) and $\mu$ an admissible twist of $K$ (an idele class character that is continuous and unitary). Assume: at every finite place $v$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ which is not bad for $(K,\mu)$ — bad meaning $v$ ramifies in $K$ or $\mu$ is ramified at some prime of $K$ above $v$ — the local component `psiLoc` $\psi\,v$ has `addCharLevel` zero; and $\mu$ is not matched by any admissible idele class character $\eta$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ in the sense that $\mu$ of a uniformiser idele at an unramified $\mathfrak{P}$ equals $\eta$ of the uniformiser idele below, raised to the inertia degree. Let $D$, $U$, `gen` be the data out of which `productionPinsOf` builds carrier pins for $\mathbb{Q}$ from the adelic box, let $S$ be a finite set of finite places consisting exactly of the bad places, and let $X$ be a `CubicInductionData` (a global form, its $\psi$-Whittaker function, local and archimedean Whittaker functions, a central character, and a dual Whittaker function) satisfying `IsCubicInductionDataOn` for $K$, these pins, $\psi$, $\mu$ and $S$. Assume further: $X.\mathrm{form}$ is continuous; $X.\mathrm{whittaker}$ and $X.\mathrm{dualWhittaker}$ are continuous and gauge-majorised in the sense of `IsGaugeMajorised3`; $c$ is an inverse of the adelic-box volume; $X.\mathrm{form}\neq 0$, and at each good place with level-zero character the local Whittaker function takes the value $1$ at the identity and has the spherical torus values `HasSphericalTorusValuesAt` for the induced coefficients of $\mu$; at bad places in any finite set, the local Whittaker function is invariant under right translation by some open subgroup and lies in the cyclic subspace generated by each non-zero element of its own cyclic subspace; $E$ splits the infinite idele units into the idele units with trivial finite part; $\nu_{\mathrm{mul}}$ is a Haar measure on the infinite idele units; $v\in S$ is a distinguished bad place; $X.\mathrm{whittakerLoc}\,w\,1=1$ at the remaining places of $S$; at each $w\in S$ the right-invariant vectors in the cyclic subspace of $X.\mathrm{whittakerLoc}\,w$ under any open subgroup lie in the span of a finite set; at each $w\in S$ the local component of the central character is unitary and $X.\mathrm{whittakerLoc}\,w$ transforms under scalar matrices by it; and finally two integrability hypotheses (`hS`, `hS'`) asserting, for every admissible twist of $\mathbb{Q}$ and every adelic $g$, convergence in a right half-plane of the $S$-part integrals defining the global zeta integral and its dual counterpart against the measure `(productMeasureData ℚ S).νS`. The conclusion is: for every admissible idele class character $\tau$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ that is unramified at every finite place $p\neq v$, every integer $e$ with $\tau$ of archimedean type $(0,e)$ at every real place, and every $g_\infty\in \mathrm{GL}_3$ of the infinite adeles for which there is an admissible $\sigma'$ of archimedean type $(t,e)$ at all real places, for some $t$, with `archZeta30` $\nu_{\mathrm{mul}}$ of $h\mapsto X.\mathrm{whittakerArch}(h\,g_\infty)$ against $\sigma'\circ E$ non-zero at points of arbitrarily large real part, and for all $g,g'\in \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_v)$: there exist adelic points $y,y'$, functions $A,\tilde A:\mathbb{C}\to\mathbb{C}$ and reals $\sigma_R,\sigma_L$ such that $A$ is non-zero at points of arbitrarily large real part; for $\mathrm{Re}\,s>\sigma_R$ the global integral `globalZeta30` of $X.\mathrm{whittaker}$ with $\tau$ at $y$ (resp. $y'$) equals $A(s)$ times `localZeta30` at $v$ for $X.\mathrm{whittakerLoc}\,v$, the local component of $\tau$ and the point $g$ (resp. $g'$), taken against the multiplicative measure obtained from the self-dual Haar measure at $v$ pulled back along the inclusion of units; and for $\mathrm{Re}\,s<\sigma_L$ the same holds with $s$ replaced by $1-s$ for `globalZetaDual31` and `localZetaDual31`, with one and the same factor $\tilde A(s)$ for both pairs $(y,g)$ and $(y',g')$.
--
--   This is the place-separation step in the converse-theorem treatment of cubic induction: the global $\mathrm{GL}_3$ zeta integral of the automorphic Whittaker function is shown to equal a single local integral at the distinguished bad place $v$, times a factor $A$ (respectively $\tilde A$ on the left half-plane) that does not depend on the $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb{Q}_v)$-argument, so that two arguments $g,g'$ may be compared. It is used by [`LanglandsTunnell.CubicInduction.mul_eq_mul_localZeta30_localZetaDual31_polynomial_of_isCubicInductionDataOn_of_forall_mem_bad`](thm.html#LanglandsTunnell.CubicInduction.mul_eq_mul_localZeta30_localZetaDual31_polynomial_of_isCubicInductionDataOn_of_forall_mem_bad), where the resulting identity between the two local zeta integrals and their duals is turned into the local functional equation at $v$.
